{
  "abstract": "We study nine-coloring extensions over an eighteen-vertex biplanar graph $H$ with independence number at most two. We prove that if $e(H)\\leq90$, then adding exact-degree-nine roots inducing $K_r\\cup K_1$, $1\\leq r\\leq8$, preserves nine-colorability whenever the resulting graph is $K_9$-free. In particular, every biplanar extension with root graph $K_6\\cup K_1$ is nine-colorable, with arbitrary and possibly distinct root neighborhoods and no criticality assumption. A hand argument using the published linking lemma and representative rectangles shows that $e(H)\\leq88$ forces exactly two necessary five-cycle complement shapes. It also gives the seventeen-vertex density lower bound of 76 edges. At the ninety-edge threshold, the triangle-free complement has disjoint independent sets of orders eight and seven, leaving an independent triple or a three-vertex path. Complete normal-form arguments reduce matching repair to explicit path, two-edge-deletion and contact-switch families. Standard-library integer verifiers check 95,894 target orbits and 687,389 maximal-independent support sets, with independent full replays. We then prove a general palette-propagation criterion converting conditional matching support into arbitrary-root extension. Its support may be disconnected: a forced pair and an induced matching-covered large component still suffice. The finite verification is separated from the hand structural results. We do not determine biplanarity of the retained abstract core shapes, cover the general $K_7\\cup K_1$ profile, or resolve unrestricted Earth–Moon.",
